"Internal Error ()\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.importyeti.com/pricing\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.importyeti.com/pricing: (404) Not Found\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImportYeti - About Us (https://www.importyeti.com/about)\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.importyeti.com/about\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 277\nL0: Menu\nL1: \nL2:   * cite0†About L3:   * cite1†Contact L4:   * cite2†Login L5: [Button: Sign up free]\nL6: \nL7: # Trade data should make sense\nL8: \nL9: ImportYeti turns trillions of shipping records into clear, usable insights—so you can look up any company, see who they source from, and make your next move with confidence.\nL10: ## Trusted by companies, universities, and organizations around the world\nL11: \nL12: cite3†Image L13: \nL14: cite4†Image L15: \nL16: cite5†Image L17: \nL18: cite6†Image L19: \nL20: cite7†Image L21: \nL22: cite8†Image L23: \nL24: cite9†Image L25: \nL26: cite10†Image L27: \nL28: cite11†Image L29: \nL30: cite12†Image L31: \nL32: cite13†Image L33: \nL34: cite14†Image L35: \nL36: cite15†Image L37: \nL38: cite16†Image L39: \nL40: cite17†Image L41: \nL42: cite18†Image L43: \nL44: cite19†Image L45: \nL46: cite20†Image L47: \nL48: cite21†Image L49: \nL50: cite3†Image L51: \nL52: cite4†Image L53: \nL54: cite5†Image L55: \nL56: cite6†Image L57: \nL58: cite7†Image L59: \nL60: cite8†Image L61: \nL62: cite9†Image L63: \nL64: cite10†Image L65: \nL66: cite11†Image L67: \nL68: cite12†Image L69: \nL70: cite13†Image L71: \nL72: cite14†Image L73: \nL74: cite15†Image L75: \nL76: cite16†Image L77: \nL78: cite17†Image L79: \nL80: cite18†Image L81: \nL82: cite19†Image L83: \nL84: cite20†Image L85: cite21†Image L86: \nL87: cite22†Image L88: \nL89: cite23†Image L90: \nL91: cite24†Image L92: \nL93: cite25†Image L94: \nL95: cite26†Image L96: \nL97: cite27†Image L98: \nL99: cite28†Image L100: \nL101: cite29†Image L102: \nL103: cite30†Image L104: \nL105: cite31†Image L106: \nL107: cite32†Image L108: \nL109: cite33†Image L110: \nL111: cite34†Image L112: \nL113: cite35†Image L114: \nL115: cite36†Image L116: \nL117: cite37†Image L118: \nL119: cite38†Image L120: \nL121: cite39†Image L122: \nL123: cite40†Image L124: \nL125: cite41†Image L126: \nL127: cite42†Image L128: \nL129: cite22†Image L130: \nL131: cite23†Image L132: \nL133: cite24†Image L134: \nL135: cite25†Image L136: \nL137: cite26†Image L138: \nL139: cite27†Image L140: \nL141: cite28†Image L142: \nL143: cite29†Image L144: \nL145: cite30†Image L146: \nL147: cite31†Image L148: \nL149: cite32†Image L150: \nL151: cite33†Image L152: \nL153: cite34†Image L154: \nL155: cite35†Image L156: \nL157: cite36†Image L158: \nL159: cite37†Image L160: \nL161: cite38†Image L162: \nL163: cite39†Image L164: \nL165: cite40†Image L166: \nL167: cite41†Image L168: cite42†Image L169: ## People Behind the Data\nL170: \nL171: We solve complex problems with clarity and care.\nL172: \nL173: cite43†Image: David Applegate L174: \nL175: ### David Applegate\nL176: \nL177: CEO / Founder\nL178: \nL179: cite44†Image: James Brewer L180: \nL181: ### James Brewer\nL182: \nL183: CRO / Founder\nL184: \nL185: cite45†Meet the full team L186: \nL187: ## Our Values\nL188: \nL189: The principles guiding our work.\nL190: \nL191: cite46†Image: Brain Learn L192: \nL193: #### First Sharpen The Axe\nL194: \nL195: Invest in learning before doing.\nL196: \nL197: cite47†Image: People L198: \nL199: #### Start With The Human\nL200: \nL201: Who it helps comes before what we build.\nL202: \nL203: cite48†Image: Bomber L204: #### Build Like Skunkworks\nL205: \nL206: Small, fast, and unafraid to experiment.\nL207: \nL208: cite49†Image: Alpha L209: \nL210: #### Seek α\nL211: \nL212: Find the edge others miss.\nL213: \nL214: cite50†Image: Diamond L215: \nL216: #### Durability > Vanity\nL217: \nL218: Built to last, not just to impress.\nL219: \nL220: 0 trillion+\nL221: \nL222: Trade data points organized by intelligent systems\nL223: \nL224: 0 million+\nL225: \nL226: Monthly users\nL227: \nL228: 0\nL229: \nL230: Updates processed per second\nL231: \nL232: ## Our Story\nL233: ### March 19, 2020 — Day 0\nL234: \nL235: California locked down. At the time, I was running WrestlingMart, the world’s largest specialty eCommerce store for collegiate wrestling gear. That is a real niche—and a terrible one to be in during a pandemic. The last industry coming back was always going to be the one where people roll around on each other sweating.\nL236: At the same time, I had spent ten years dealing with complex supply chain problems. Every container hitting a U.S. port leaves a paper trail. Large importers and operators knew this and used the data constantly—but for everyone else it was locked behind expensive subscriptions, clunky government databases, and software that felt built to repel normal people. The data was public; it just was not accessible. That felt wrong.\nL237: ### April 15, 2020 — Day 27\nL238: \nL239: Twenty-seven days later, I shipped the first version of ImportYeti.\nL240: \nL241: I posted it to Reddit's FBA community with the title: \"Free Bill of Lading Database.\"\nL242: \nL243: People tore the product apart—ugly UI, dirty data, brutal comments. But that was the signal: nobody writes angry feedback about a product they do not want to exist.\nL244: \nL245: So I spent the next week DMing critics, thanking them, and fixing issues.\nL246: ### 2020–2021\nL247: \nL248: Then the strange part happened: people showed up. Because the world was stuck inside, users were unusually willing to talk—I ended up having over 1,000 Zoom calls with importers, operators, and eCommerce founders. They showed me exactly where the product broke, where the data was confusing, and what actually mattered, and that period shaped everything.\nL249: We proved people wanted this, that we could build it, and that there was a business here. Along the way we hired our first team members; Andrey joined early and still helps lead engineering today.\nL250: ### 2021–2022\nL251: \nL252: ImportYeti kept growing: we crossed 1,000,000 users, then 2,000,000; launched Power Query so people could work with the data the way professionals do; and reached cash flow positive.\nL253: \nL254: No VC, no growth-at-all-costs—just a sustainable business built by listening closely and shipping aggressively.\nL255: ### Today\nL256: \nL257: There was no single magic moment after that—we kept doing the work: listening to users, building what mattered, ignoring what did not, and trying to make global trade data easier to use for the people who actually need it.\nL258: \nL259: That is still the job, and we are still doing it.\nL260: ## We want to hear your thoughts...\nL261: \nL262: If you enjoyed ImportYeti or have any ideas for how to improve it, I want to hear from you.\nL263: \nL264: cite51†Image: David Applegate L265: \nL266: cite52†Image: David Applegate signature L267: \nL268: David Applegate\nL269: \nL270: ImportYeti's Founder\nL271: \nL272: cite53†[email protected] L273: \nL274: cite0†About cite54†Press cite1†Contact cite55†Privacy cite56†FAQs cite57†Pricing L275: \nL276: [Input: Find Any Company's Suppliers]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTerms of Service (https://www.census.gov/data/developers/about/terms-of-service.html)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view5\",\"lineno\":96}); Total lines: 206\nL0: cite0†Skip Header L1: \nL2: About\nL3: \nL4: ci